---
# The bounds (MAX_MSGS, MAX_TROUBLES) end every behaviour in a quiet
# thread with nothing left to do: that is rest, not a deadlock.
deadlock_detection: false
---

# The composer's queue draining into a refusal, as one tab sees it
# (web/src/app.tsx Thread: enqueue, the flush effect, deliver() and its
# failure rows, Retry/Edit/Discard on a row; App: deliverTo, archiveRow;
# serve: API.prompt's 409 on a pending ask, Supervisor.Send's
# ErrArchived, SetArchived).
#
# A queued message waits in the tab until the turn ends; the flush effect
# then hands its head to deliver(). That POST can be refused: the session
# was archived (here, or in another tab this one has not polled yet),
# serve is down, or a question (tools.ask) armed between the flush's
# decision and the POST. The refused head becomes a "Not sent" failure
# row naming its cause, and the queue behind it stops until the cause
# goes away.
#
# THIS SPEC IS THE INTENDED BEHAVIOUR, NOT TODAY'S. Read against main
# (app.tsx at 5471918e) the page departs from it in four places; each is
# named at the step or assertion it breaks, and the MBT/browser stages
# are expected to go red on them:
# 1. The flush guard has no row.archived check, and flushing.current is
#    reset on every failures.length change: after an archive every
#    queued message is posted, refused with 409 and turned into a
#    failure, one per render (Flush; OneRefusalPerTrouble).
# 2. Nothing holds the queue after a network failure, so with serve
#    down the same cascade empties the queue into failures (Flush,
#    `offline`; OneRefusalPerTrouble).
# 3. Retry calls deliver() at once: while a turn runs it is a steer, so
#    a retried message lands in the middle of the turn a younger queued
#    message started (Retry; RetryWaitsItsTurn).
# 4. A retry that fails again is appended at the end of the failure
#    rows, not in its place (Post; FailuresInOrder). Out of reach at
#    these bounds: it takes a third refusal (two rows, then the older
#    one retried into a third cause), and MAX_TROUBLES = 3 is ~2200
#    states. The insert by id is kept so the claim reads right.
#
# Fields (all on the Session role):
#   status    the row's status: running | idle | needs-you (an armed
#             ask). The tab's view equals the server's except for
#             `archived` (below); a row the page cannot poll (serve
#             down) does not change here, so the turn only ends while
#             serve is up.
#   archived  meta.Archived on the server.
#   seen_archived  row.archived as this tab has it: set at once when this
#             tab archives, after a poll (or the refresh after a refused
#             send) when another tab did.
#   serve     "up" | "down".
#   offline   this tab saw a request fail on the network and holds its
#             queue until serve answers again.
#   queue     queued message ids, oldest first (sessionStorage).
#   inflight  the id whose POST the flush (or nothing) has outstanding,
#             -1 when none. The flushing ref of app.tsx.
#   failures  failure rows' message ids, oldest first.
#   causes    each failure row's cause, parallel to `failures`:
#             "archived" (409 ErrArchived), "ask" (409 pending ask),
#             "network" (fetch failed).
#   draft     a failure row was put back in the composer by Edit.
#   made      messages written so far; the next id and a bound.
#   troubles  refusal causes started (archive here or elsewhere, serve
#             down, the racing ask); a bound, and the budget refusals
#             are counted against. Tracked by the client (adapter).
#   refused   refused POSTs so far. Tracked by the client (adapter).
#
# Where this departs from the mapped model, and why:
# - "ChildExit after Kill" stays a step of its own: SetArchived saves
#   the flag and then kills, and the row can say running (with the
#   archived flag) until the child is reaped. The flush guard holds on
#   `running` in that window anyway. Whether an idle child is still up
#   is not a field: nothing on the page shows it, and it doubled the
#   graph. "Unarchive never spawns a child" is read off the row instead:
#   unarchiving changes neither the status nor what is in flight.
# - The ask race is a turn another client starts (or a steer the pump
#   made a turn) that asks while this tab's flushed POST is on its way:
#   the only way an ask can arm while the tab saw the row idle.
# - "serve_back" and "retry_in_flight" are not fields: ServeBack clears
#   `offline`, and a retry goes back to the queue's front and is then
#   flushed like any queued message (`inflight`).
# - Answers (failure rows with answer=true) are left out: the queue
#   never holds an answer.

MAX_MSGS = 2
MAX_TROUBLES = 2

role Session:
    # A turn is already running (its prompt is not what this flow is
    # about), so messages can be queued behind it.
    action Init:
        self.status = "running"
        self.archived = False
        self.seen_archived = False
        self.serve = "up"
        self.offline = False
        self.queue = []
        self.inflight = -1
        self.failures = []
        self.causes = []
        self.draft = False
        self.made = 0
        self.troubles = 0
        self.refused = 0

    # ---- the person ----

    # Cmd/Ctrl+Enter or Queue while the turn runs. With an edited failure
    # in the composer, that text is what is queued (as a new message).
    atomic action Enqueue:
        require self.status == "running" and not self.seen_archived
        require self.made < MAX_MSGS
        self.queue = self.queue + [self.made]
        self.made = self.made + 1
        self.draft = False

    # Remove on the newest queued row. Rows are acted on at fixed places
    # (this one, and the oldest failure row below), not through a oneof:
    # fizzbee-mbt checks a step against the fork's intermediate node, and
    # the forks alone pushed the graph past 1900 nodes.
    atomic action RemoveQueued:
        require len(self.queue) > 0
        self.queue = self.queue[:-1]

    # Retry on the oldest failure row: the message rejoins the queue in
    # its place, ahead of every younger one, and waits its turn there. It
    # is never posted past the one-per-turn rule (a steer into a turn a
    # younger message started). Today it is posted at once (departure 3).
    atomic fair action Retry:
        require len(self.failures) > 0
        m = self.failures[0]
        j = len([q for q in self.queue if q < m])
        self.queue = self.queue[:j] + [m] + self.queue[j:]
        self.failures = self.failures[1:]
        self.causes = self.causes[1:]

    # Edit on the oldest failure row: the text back in an empty
    # composer, the row gone.
    atomic fair action Edit:
        require len(self.failures) > 0 and not self.draft
        self.failures = self.failures[1:]
        self.causes = self.causes[1:]
        self.draft = True

    atomic fair action Discard:
        require len(self.failures) > 0
        self.failures = self.failures[1:]
        self.causes = self.causes[1:]

    atomic action ClearDraft:
        require self.draft
        self.draft = False

    # Archive... in this tab: the row is marked at once (archiveRow's
    # mark) and serve saves the flag, then kills the child.
    atomic action ArchiveHere:
        require not self.archived and not self.seen_archived
        require self.serve == "up" and self.troubles < MAX_TROUBLES
        self.archived = True
        self.seen_archived = True
        self.troubles = self.troubles + 1

    # Another tab (or the CLI) archives: this tab learns it at its next
    # poll, or from the refresh after a refused send.
    atomic action ArchiveElsewhere:
        require not self.archived and not self.seen_archived
        require self.serve == "up" and self.troubles < MAX_TROUBLES
        self.archived = True
        self.troubles = self.troubles + 1

    # Unarchive (only offered on a row the tab shows archived). It only
    # clears the flag: nothing is spawned until a message is accepted.
    atomic action Unarchive:
        require self.archived and self.seen_archived and self.serve == "up"
        self.archived = False
        self.seen_archived = False

    atomic action ServeDown:
        require self.serve == "up" and self.troubles < MAX_TROUBLES
        self.serve = "down"
        self.troubles = self.troubles + 1

    # A turn another client started asks while this tab's flushed POST
    # is on its way (the ask race).
    atomic action AskArrives:
        require self.status == "idle" and self.inflight >= 0
        require not self.archived and self.serve == "up"
        require self.troubles < MAX_TROUBLES
        self.status = "needs-you"
        self.troubles = self.troubles + 1

    # The question is answered (here or elsewhere); its turn runs on.
    atomic action AnswerAsk:
        require self.status == "needs-you" and self.serve == "up"
        require not self.archived
        self.status = "running"

    # ---- the page, serve and the child ----

    # The flush effect: one queued message per ended turn. Held while the
    # tab shows the row archived and while it knows serve is unreachable
    # (departures 1 and 2: today neither holds it).
    atomic fair action Flush:
        require self.status == "idle" and self.inflight == -1
        require len(self.queue) > 0
        require not self.seen_archived and not self.offline
        self.inflight = self.queue[0]
        self.queue = self.queue[1:]

    # The POST's answer, then deliverTo's refresh. Serve refuses in this
    # order: unreachable, a pending ask (API.prompt checks it before
    # anything else), archived (Send -> ensure).
    atomic fair action Post:
        require self.inflight >= 0
        m = self.inflight
        self.inflight = -1
        cause = ""
        if self.serve == "down":
            cause = "network"
            self.offline = True
        elif self.status == "needs-you":
            cause = "ask"
        elif self.archived:
            cause = "archived"
            self.seen_archived = True
        else:
            self.status = "running"
        if cause != "":
            # A failure row goes in by id, so a retried message that
            # fails again takes its old place among the rows.
            i = len([f for f in self.failures if f < m])
            self.failures = self.failures[:i] + [m] + self.failures[i:]
            self.causes = self.causes[:i] + [cause] + self.causes[i:]
            self.refused = self.refused + 1

    atomic fair action TurnEnds:
        require self.status == "running" and self.serve == "up"
        require not self.archived
        self.status = "idle"

    # The archived session's child is reaped; its turn (or question) ends
    # with it.
    atomic fair action ChildExit:
        require self.archived and self.status != "idle"
        self.status = "idle"

    # This tab's poll catches up with an archive (or unarchive) it did
    # not make.
    atomic fair action Poll:
        require self.serve == "up" and self.seen_archived != self.archived
        self.seen_archived = self.archived

    # Serve answers again; the tab's next poll sees it and lets the
    # queue go.
    atomic fair action ServeBack:
        require self.serve == "down"
        self.serve = "up"
        self.offline = False

action Init:
    session = Session()

# While the tab shows the row archived, or knows serve is down, the
# queue is never flushed; and never into a running turn or an ask.
transition assertion FlushOnlyWhenFree(before, after):
    if after.session.inflight >= 0 and before.session.inflight == -1:
        if before.session.seen_archived or before.session.offline:
            return False
        return before.session.status == "idle"
    return True

# A refusal stops the queue: each cause (an archive seen here or not, a
# serve outage, a racing ask) costs at most one refused request, so a
# tab that has not seen archived=true sends at most one, and the rest of
# the queue stays queued.
always assertion OneRefusalPerTrouble:
    return session.refused <= session.troubles

# The refused head is the only thing a refusal turns into a failure row.
transition assertion RefusalKeepsQueue(before, after):
    if len(after.session.failures) > len(before.session.failures):
        if len(after.session.failures) != len(before.session.failures) + 1:
            return False
        return after.session.queue == before.session.queue
    return True

# Failure rows are in message order and each names its cause.
always assertion FailuresInOrder:
    if len(session.failures) != len(session.causes):
        return False
    for c in session.causes:
        if c not in ("archived", "ask", "network"):
            return False
    for i in range(len(session.failures) - 1):
        if session.failures[i] >= session.failures[i + 1]:
            return False
    return True

# The queue is in message order: a retried message goes back in front of
# the younger ones still queued behind it.
always assertion QueueInOrder:
    for i in range(len(session.queue) - 1):
        if session.queue[i] >= session.queue[i + 1]:
            return False
    return True

# Retry never posts by itself, and the message goes back ahead of every
# younger message still queued.
transition assertion RetryWaitsItsTurn(before, after):
    back = [m for m in before.session.failures
            if m in after.session.queue or m == after.session.inflight]
    if back:
        m = back[0]
        if after.session.inflight == m:
            return False
        seen = False
        for q in after.session.queue:
            if q == m:
                seen = True
            elif q > m and not seen:
                return False
    return True

# Unarchive never spawns a child or sends anything by itself: the row's
# status and what is in flight are what they were.
transition assertion UnarchiveSpawnsNothing(before, after):
    if before.session.archived and not after.session.archived:
        if after.session.inflight != before.session.inflight:
            return False
        return after.session.status == before.session.status
    return True

# Liveness: once serve is back, the row unarchived (and seen so) and the
# ask answered, the queue drains.
always eventually assertion QueueDrains:
    return ((len(session.queue) == 0 and session.inflight == -1)
            or session.archived or session.seen_archived
            or session.status == "needs-you")

# Every failure row is eventually retried, edited or discarded.
always eventually assertion FailuresResolve:
    return len(session.failures) == 0
